    "/v1/devices/{device_id}": {
      "delete": {
        "summary": "Revoke Device",
        "description": "Kill one credential. A device that is not there is a 404.\n\nNot a silent 200: this is the control somebody reaches for when they think\na device is compromised, and \"nothing was revoked\" has to be\ndistinguishable from \"it is gone now\". The distinction discloses nothing a\ncaller holding SYSTEM_CONTROL on the loopback listener could not read\nstraight off `GET /v1/devices` a line earlier.\n\nA third outcome, 503, is what `TokenVault.revoke()` raising\n`VaultUnavailableError` becomes -- either half of it. If the read fails,\ndevices.json could not be read at all, so whether `device_id` exists is\ngenuinely unknown; answering 404 there would tell the one person trying\nto cut a device off that it is already gone when the truth is \"ask again\nonce the file is readable\" -- exactly the control that must not lie\nunder duress. If the *write* fails instead (the same Windows lock\ncontention, on the save half), the underlying `PermissionError` would\notherwise reach `errors.py`'s app-wide mapping and answer 403 \"protected\npath\" -- which here would read as \"you are not allowed to revoke this\ndevice\", the same lie the 404 case argues against, just from the other\ndirection. Both halves become 503: unavailable, not unauthorised and not\nalready gone.",
        "operationId": "revoke_device_v1_devices__device_id__delete",
        "parameters": [
          {
            "name": "device_id",
            "in": "path",
            "required": true,
            "schema": {
              "type": "string",
              "title": "Device Id"
            }
          }
        ],
        "responses": {
          "200": {
            "description": "Successful Response",
            "content": {
              "application/json": {
                "schema": {
                  "$ref": "#/components/schemas/Envelope_RevokedPayload_"
                }
              }
            }
          },
          "422": {
            "description": "Validation Error",
            "content": {
              "application/json": {
                "schema": {
                  "$ref": "#/components/schemas/HTTPValidationError"
                }
              }
            }
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "/v1/devices/{device_id}/raise": {
      "post": {
        "summary": "Raise Device Ceiling",
        "description": "Let one device do more, on one transport, for a bounded while.\n\nThree bounds stack and only the innermost one moves. What the device was\nissued is never widened -- a raise lifts a transport's refusal, it cannot\nmanufacture a grant. `ceiling | raisable` is static module data vetted once\nby a human, and `raisable` is empty on every listener but `tailnet`, which\nis what makes the public tunnels unraisable by construction rather than by\na check somebody could forget. Only the live record this route mints is\nnew, and it expires on a clock the caller does not control.\n\nRefused with distinct statuses, because each one is a different thing for\nthe operator to fix: 404 when the device does not exist, 422 when a\ncapability name is not one, 403 when the transport was never vetted to\ncarry it or the device was never issued it, and 409 when the transport is\nnot currently running -- a raise scoped to a listener that is down can\nnever be exercised, and must not be left behind for a future listener that\nreuses the name.\n\nAn over-long request is clamped to the seven-day cap rather than refused.\nThe cap is the safety property, not a promise the caller kept its word.",
        "operationId": "raise_device_ceiling_v1_devices__device_id__raise_post",
        "parameters": [
          {
            "name": "device_id",
            "in": "path",
            "required": true,
            "schema": {
              "type": "string",
              "title": "Device Id"
            }
          }
        ],
        "requestBody": {
          "required": true,
